    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d159">

                <pb facs="0241" n="171" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv01/pg171"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">
                    Woodrow Wilson Papers</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Secretary of the Treasury</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >McAdoo</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi>
                        </placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian" when="1918-11-25">25 November,
                            1918</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mac</hi>: Of course I realize the force of
                    the recommendation which Mr. William
                        McAdoo urges,<anchor n="29" xml:id="d159-f1"/> but I could not
                    think of appointing Mr. Root, because I have had more
                    opportunities than he has had <pb facs="0242" n="172" xml:id="pg172"/> of
                    knowing just how hopeless a reactionary he is. I think his appointment would
                    discourage every liberal element in the world.</p>
                <p>In haste,</p>
                <p>Affectionately yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>[<hi rendition="#small-caps">Woodrow
                                Wilson</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
